  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 325 AM CDT Mon Aug 17 2026\n\nAny lingering showers have since shifted southeast of the area\nin the wake of a cold front which will bring a brief break today\nfrom the active weather pattern. A reinforcing lake enhanced \nfront is currently moving down the western side of Lake Michigan\nand will drift into the area this morning. A low-level \nconvergence axis is expected to set up where this boundary \nstalls out, near and just west of Lake Michigan this morning \ninto the afternoon. Modeled soundings suggest there is a window \nwhen cloud depths may be just deep enough (paired with a ribbon \nof higher low-level instability) to support the development of a\nnarrow corridor of showers and/or sprinkles late morning into \nthe afternoon. Opted to add slight chances (15-20%) for showers \nin the gridded forecast to account for this near Lake Michigan \nand as far west as I-355. However, most areas are expected to \nremain dry today even if these showers develop.\n\nTemperatures today will be largely dependent upon which side of\nthe aforementioned convergence axis sets up, with upper 70s to \nlower 80s farther inland and low to mid 70s closer to the lake.\nDiurnal mixing to the west of this axis should also help keep \nheat indices in check compared to yesterday. Temperatures \ntonight are forecast to drop into the low to mid 60s for most of\nthe area. If high clouds are slower to drift into the area \novernight, temperatures may manage to drop into the upper 50s \nacross portions of interior northern Illinois.\n\nAttention then turns to Tuesday when a series of subtle\nwaves/vorts are forecast to move within the broader northwest \nflow aloft across the area in the afternoon and night. This may\nlead to the development of thunderstorms across southern\nWisconsin and northern Illinois in the afternoon that moves \nsoutheast across the rest of the area into the evening and \novernight hours. While there may be some capping initially to \nlimit coverage, any storms that develop will have the potential \nto become severe, especially near and north of I-80 where deep \nlayer shear will be the highest (upwards of 40 to 50 kts). The\nflooding threat may be more localized initially with a more\ndiscrete mode, however, if storms transition toward multicell \nclusters or redevelop over the same areas into the evening and \novernight hours, flash flooding would become a greater concern.\n\nThere continues to be variability amongst the available model \nguidance with respect to shower/storm coverage here locally, \nlikely owing to differences in the strength of the capping and \nposition and strength of the low-level jet. Given some of these \nuncertainties, SPC and WPC have maintained a level 1 of 5 \nthreat for severe weather and level 1 of 4 threat for flash \nflooding respectively. However, am becoming a bit more concerned\nthat a conditionally higher severe and flash flooding threat \nmay unfold over at least portions of the area and will continue \nto message this potential. Will have to monitor trends closely \nover the next 24 hours.\n\nAnother break in this active pattern is in store Wednesday into\nThursday as a cooler and less humid airmass settles into the \narea in the wake of a cold front late Tuesday night/early \nWednesday AM. Temperatures will be cooler (typically lower 80s \nfor mid August) with highs in the mid to upper 70s each day \namidst cooler onshore flow. Confidence in the details then \ndecreases late week into the upcoming weekend as additional \nwaves move within the upper level northwest flow pattern, with a\npotentially more robust and slower moving wave bringing \nincreasing shower/storm chances for the weekend.\n\nPetr",
